Launchpad PPA — publishing flow
================================

This document describes how to publish a tagged release to the
Launchpad Personal Package Archive (PPA) at
`ppa:mgajda/duckdb-deb`.

Two audiences:
  1. END USERS who want a one-line `apt install duckdb` via PPA.
  2. MAINTAINERS who run debian/scripts/publish-ppa.sh.

------------------------------------------------------------
1. End users — using the PPA
------------------------------------------------------------

Note: as of writing, this PPA is **not yet active** — the
publishing flow is drafted but the first successful upload has
not happened.  Once uploads start, this section will be the
recommended install path for Ubuntu users (alongside the
GitHub-Pages apt repo for Debian users).

When live, install with:

    sudo add-apt-repository ppa:mgajda/duckdb-deb
    sudo apt-get update
    sudo apt-get install duckdb libduckdb-dev

`add-apt-repository` fetches the PPA's signing key from
keyserver.ubuntu.com automatically; no curl-and-dearmor dance.

Supported Ubuntu series (PPA-side):

    jammy   (22.04 LTS)
    noble   (24.04 LTS)

A Debian series can use the GitHub-Pages apt repo instead
(see debian/README.apt-pages); PPAs are Ubuntu-only on the
Launchpad side, even though the same source compiles under
Debian and Ubuntu both.

Removing the PPA:

    sudo add-apt-repository --remove ppa:mgajda/duckdb-deb
    sudo apt-get update

------------------------------------------------------------
2. Maintainers — uploading a release
------------------------------------------------------------

The PPA model differs from the GitHub-Pages apt repo in one
crucial way: **Launchpad rebuilds the source package server-side**.
You upload only the signed `_source.changes` + `.dsc` + orig
tarball.  Launchpad's autobuilders then produce the .debs on
*their* infrastructure, for *every* architecture they support.

This is a substantial win:
  - amd64, arm64, ppc64el, s390x, armhf builds for free
  - No CI runner cost on our side
  - Launchpad's autobuilders are the reference for Debian/Ubuntu
    archive admission, so a green PPA build is a strong signal
    that Debian mentors would accept the same source.

The cost is turnaround time: a Launchpad build takes 20-60 min
per (series, arch), and queues may delay the start by hours.

Prerequisites (one-time)
------------------------

1. Launchpad account at https://launchpad.net/ — use your real
   name; Launchpad's PPAs are tied to identity.  Note your
   username; for this packaging it is `mgajda`.

2. Upload your GPG public key to Launchpad:
       https://launchpad.net/~/+editpgpkeys
   Launchpad emails you a key-encrypted challenge; decrypt and
   submit the response.  Until you do this, dput uploads will be
   rejected.

3. Sign the Launchpad Code of Conduct (one click):
       https://launchpad.net/codeofconduct

4. Activate the PPA:
       https://launchpad.net/~mgajda/+activate-ppa
   Name it `duckdb-deb` so the upload target is
   `ppa:mgajda/duckdb-deb`.  In the description, link back to
   github.com/mgajda/duckdb-deb so end users can find the source.

5. Install tooling:
       sudo apt-get install dput devscripts ubuntu-dev-tools gnupg

Uploading a tag
---------------

After CI has produced a green build for the tag and you've
created a GitHub Release:

    debian/scripts/publish-ppa.sh v<X.Y.Z>-deb<N>

The script:
  - Checks out the tag in a temporary worktree per Ubuntu series
  - Runs dch to set the per-series version (e.g. ~jammy0)
  - Generates the orig tarball
  - Runs `dpkg-buildpackage -S -sa -k<KEY>` to sign the source
    (you'll be prompted to unlock gpg-agent)
  - PRINTS the dput commands but does NOT execute them

You then copy-paste the printed dput commands once your
ssh-agent / gpg-agent is unlocked.  This separation is
intentional: chaining `dput` to a possibly-locked agent inside
the script is a recipe for retry loops and confusing errors.

Typical printed output:

    dput ppa:mgajda/duckdb-deb /home/m/.cache/duckdb-ppa-v1.5.2-deb1/jammy/duckdb_1.5.2~jammy0_source.changes
    dput ppa:mgajda/duckdb-deb /home/m/.cache/duckdb-ppa-v1.5.2-deb1/noble/duckdb_1.5.2~noble0_source.changes

Monitoring the build
--------------------

After dput, Launchpad emails you "Accepted" (within ~1 min) or
"Rejected" (with reason).  Then the build queue:

    https://launchpad.net/~mgajda/+archive/ubuntu/duckdb-deb/+packages

Per-build status flows through:
  Pending  ->  Currently building  ->  Successfully built  ->  Published
                                  -> Failed to build

Programmatic polling via the REST API (no auth needed for reads):

    curl -sH "Accept: application/json" \
        "https://api.launchpad.net/1.0/~mgajda/+archive/ubuntu/duckdb-deb?ws.op=getBuildSummaries"

The same shape your CI watcher uses.

If a build fails
----------------

Inspect the build log in the Launchpad UI.  Common failure modes:

  - **Build-dep not in main**: the PPA only has access to packages
    in main + universe.  If we ever declare a Build-Depends on a
    package in restricted or multiverse, the build fails here.
    Check the version of each Build-Depend with `apt-cache policy
    <pkg>` on a clean Ubuntu install.

  - **Wrong distribution field**: dput rejects an upload to a PPA
    whose Distribution: in the .changes doesn't match a series the
    PPA accepts.  publish-ppa.sh sets this per-series; manual
    uploads need `dch -D <series> --force-distribution`.

  - **Already uploaded**: each (source-name, version) tuple can be
    uploaded ONCE.  To re-upload, bump the version (e.g.
    `~jammy0` -> `~jammy1`).  This is intentional Launchpad
    behaviour to prevent silent overwrites.

  - **Missing orig tarball on re-upload**: the FIRST upload of a
    new upstream version includes the orig tarball (we pass
    `-sa` to dpkg-buildpackage); subsequent uploads for the same
    upstream version DO NOT (dput uploads only the .dsc + diff +
    .changes).  publish-ppa.sh passes -sa unconditionally; dput
    will silently de-dup on the server side.

Key rotation
------------

The gpg key Launchpad knows about is the one whose UID matches
your account's primary email.  When you generate a new key:

  1. Generate the new key with the same UID as the old one
  2. Sign the new key with the old one
  3. Upload the new key to Launchpad (~/+editpgpkeys)
  4. Wait ~1 hour for Launchpad to verify the cross-signature
  5. Re-publish with the new key as gpg's default
  6. Mark the old key as "Deactivated" in Launchpad (this stops
     accepting NEW uploads signed by it; previously-accepted
     uploads stay valid)

------------------------------------------------------------
3. Relationship to other channels
------------------------------------------------------------

This packaging maintains three distribution channels, in
publish order:

  1. GitHub Pages apt repo  (debian/README.apt-pages)
     -- ships from CI .debs, signed by maintainer's GPG key,
        amd64+arm64 only, Debian+Ubuntu

  2. Launchpad PPA          (this document)
     -- ships from source, rebuilt by Launchpad, all archs the
        Launchpad builders support, Ubuntu only

  3. OBS apt repo           (debian/README.OBS, planned)
     -- ships from source, rebuilt by OBS, Debian+Ubuntu+others

Per the publish-flow memory, channels are exercised in this
order: CI green -> GitHub release -> Pages apt repo -> PPA after
field-testing -> OBS once account is active.  Each step is a
deliberate human-gated decision.

------------------------------------------------------------
4. Comparison: PPA vs OBS
------------------------------------------------------------

Both ship from source, both rebuild server-side.  Differences:

  PPA (Launchpad):
    + Free, Canonical-hosted, well-known to Ubuntu users
    + `add-apt-repository` UX is one command
    - Ubuntu-only (no Debian/Mint/etc. official path)
    - Slow queue (sometimes hours)
    - Build infrastructure visibility ends at build-failure
      logs; no shell access

  OBS (openSUSE Build Service):
    + Multi-distro: Debian, Ubuntu, Fedora, openSUSE, RHEL ...
    + Faster queue
    + Mature build-graph tooling
    - Less well-known to Ubuntu users
    - Adding the repo is a curl-and-dearmor song, not one command
    - The account approval process is slow (we hit this here)

We use both because they complement each other.
